mill-liberty-2.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-2.logic.r1 has "self_chooses → all_faculties"; mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.2 and mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "he chooses his plan of life for himself" is true, which the second allows
mill-liberty-2.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-2.logic.r1, to: Suppose "he chooses his plan of life for himself" is true and "he employs all his faculties (observation, reasoning, judgment, activity, discrimination, firmness and self-control)" is false. Is S3, "He must use observation to see, reasoning and judgment to foresee, activity to gather materials for decision, discrimination to decide, and when he has decided, firmness and self-control to hold to his deliberate decision.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
mill-liberty-2.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-2.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.3 have "◇(guided_well ∧ ¬all_faculties)"; mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.2 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
mill-liberty-2.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S5 is read mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.2 with no modality; mill-liberty-2.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "might" does not say which it means
mill-liberty-2.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-2.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "possibly, (he is guided in some good path and kept out of harm's way and it is not so that he employs all his faculties (observation, reasoning, judgment, activity, discrimination, firmness and self-control))" and "possibly, (he is guided in some good path and kept out of harm's way and it is not so that he employs all his faculties (observation, reasoning, judgment, activity, discrimination, firmness and self-control))" are false. Is S5, "It is possible that he might be guided in some good path, and kept out of harm's way, without any of these things.", true then?
mill-liberty-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: mill-liberty-2.logic.r1 has "manner_matters ∧ only_imitation → lower_worth"; mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.2 has "only_imitation → ¬all_faculties" and "manner_matters ∧ ¬all_faculties → lower_worth"; they differ when only "he has no need of any other faculty than the ape-like one of imitation", "he employs all his faculties (observation, reasoning, judgment, activity, discrimination, firmness and self-control)", "it is of importance not only what men do but also what manner of men they are that do it" and "his comparative worth as a human being is lessened" are true, which the first allows; mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.3 has "manner_matters ∧ man_first ∧ nature_grows → (only_imitation → lower_worth)"
mill-liberty-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.2 takes for granted "if he has no need of any other faculty than the ape-like one of imitation, then it is not so that he employs all his faculties (observation, reasoning, judgment, activity, discrimination, firmness and self-control)"; mill-liberty-2.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.3 do not
mill-liberty-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if he has no need of any other faculty than the ape-like one of imitation, then it is not so that he employs all his faculties (observation, reasoning, judgment, activity, discrimination, firmness and self-control)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
mill-liberty-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.2 takes for granted "if (it is of importance not only what men do but also what manner of men they are that do it and it is not so that he employs all his faculties (observation, reasoning, judgment, activity, discrimination, firmness and self-control)), then his comparative worth as a human being is lessened"; mill-liberty-2.logic.r1 and mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.3 do not [in 1 of 2 runs]
mill-liberty-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps mill-liberty-2.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (it is of importance not only what men do but also what manner of men they are that do it and it is not so that he employs all his faculties (observation, reasoning, judgment, activity, discrimination, firmness and self-control)), then his comparative worth as a human being is lessened"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 2 runs]
